## ONLINE METHODS
Generation of midbrain dopamine neurons from opsin-expressing hESC lines. Induction of the ventral midbrain dopamine (mesDA) fate was induced as described previously2 with the omission of FGF8. Briefly, neuralization was initiated $24\mathrm{~h~}$ after plating $1.5\times10^{5}\mathrm{hESCs}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (designated as day 0) onto Matrigel-coated plates (1:50, Life Technologies) by dual SMAD inhibi­ tion46 using LDN193189 $\mathrm{100\nM}$ , Stemgent) and SB431542 $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , Tocris) in knockout serum replacement (KSR, Life Technologies) containing media. Ventralization was initiated by adding SHH C25II $\mathrm{100ng/ml}$ , R&D, days 1–6) and Purmorphamine $2~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , Stemgent, days 1–6) as well as CHIR99021 ( $3\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , Stemgent, days 3–12). From day 5 to day 10, knockout serum replace­ ment (KSR) containing medium was replaced with N2 medium46. Media was changed to a 1:1 mixture of DMEM:F12 medium with N2 and Neurobasal medium with B27 (all Life Technologies) with CHIR (until day 13), ascorbic acid ( $\mathrm{.}0.2\ \mathrm{mM}$ , Sigma), brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF, $20~\mathrm{ng/ml}$ ; R&D), glial cell line–derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF, $20~\mathrm{ng/ml}$ ; R&D), di­butyryl cAMP ( $0.2\mathrm{mM}$ ; Sigma), transforming growth factor type β3 ${\mathrm{TGF}}\upbeta3$ , $\mathrm{{1\ng/ml};}$ R&D) and DAPT $5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ ; Tocris) on day 11. On day 20, cells were passaged by incubation with Accutase (Life Technologies) and re-plated $\mathrm{\dot{4}}\times10^{5}$ cells per $\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) on dishes coated with polyornithine (PO; $15\:\upmu\mathrm{g/ml},$ )/ laminin $\mathrm{~(1~\ug/ml)~}$ /fibronectin $(2~\mathrm{\upmu{g}/m l})$ in differentiation medium (DMEMF12/N2 and NB/B27 + ascorbic acid, BDNF, GDNF, dbcAMP, $\mathrm{TGF}\upbeta3$ and DAPT) and fed twice per week thereafter.
Calcium imaging. Transgene-expressing mesDA-rich cells were plated onto PO/laminin/fibronectin-coated $40\mathrm{-mm}$ round glass coverslips in droplets of $5\times10^{4}\mathrm{cells}/20\upmu\mathrm{l}$ on day 30 and used for calcium imaging as described pre­ viously47 between day 60 and day 100. Cultures were incubated with $5~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ Fura-2 (Life Technologies) for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then mounted in an FCS2 imaging chamber (Bioptechs). Cultures were perfused with normal Tyrodes’s saline containing (in mM) NaCl 125, KCl 5, $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ , glucose 30, HEPES 25 and bovine serum albumin $0.1\%$ w/v, pH 7.4 and supplemented with gluta­ mate (50 or $100\upmu\mathrm{M}\backslash$ ) or KCl ( $55~\mathrm{mM}$ , $\mathrm{NaCl}75~\mathrm{mM},$ ). Cultures were imaged using a $40\times1.3$ numerical aperture oil immersion objective (Zeiss) every 5 s at 340 and $380~\mathrm{nm}$ and illuminated repetitively for optogenetic testing for $15\mathrm{-}120~\mathrm{s}$ at $550~\mathrm{nm}$ with light intensities ranging from $2{-}7.5\ \mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ on an Axiovert $200~\mathrm{M}$ Inverted Microscope (Zeiss) equipped with Metamorph Software (Molecular Devices) for ratiometric analysis. A minimum of three cultures from independent differentiations were analyzed for every experimen­ tal condition. Statistical analysis for ∆F/F between EYFP and HALO cultures in response to illumination was performed using unpaired two-tailed t-test. Error bars represent s.e.m.
HPLC. For dopamine measurement experiments, transgene-expressing cells were plated onto PO/laminin/fibronectin-coated 24-well plates in droplets of $2\times10^{5}$ cells $20\ \upmu\mathrm{l},$ 5 mm diameter) on day 30 and used between day 90 and day 100. HPLC with electrochemical detection (HPLC-EC) was done as previously described36. Briefly, before supernatant collection, cells were incubated in fresh DMEM:F12 + N2 media for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . After exposure to either Tyrode’s saline alone or supplemented with high KCl $55\mathrm{mM}$ , Sigma) or glutamate $(100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , Sigma) for $10\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature and dim ambient light (approximately $300\mathrm{{lx}}$ ), supernatant was collected and immediately mixed with perchloric acid $_{\mathrm{~\tiny~0.1~M~}}$ final concentration) to deproteinize the sample and prevent dopamine auto-oxidation. For optogenetic inhibition, a $543\mathrm{-nm}$ diode pumped solid-state pigtailed laser (OEM Laser) was used during secreta­ gogue application at an output power of $100\mathrm{mW}$ to achieve an illumination of $4\mathrm{mW/mm}^{2}$ over the entire culture area. Supernatant samples were sonicated at room temperature for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , centrifuged at $_{10,000g}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , stored at $-80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and analyzed within the following 2 weeks by reversed phase HPLC-EC. The investigator performing HPLC measurements was blinded to sample identities. Cells in each sample were collected to normalize for pro­ tein content. DA concentrations in each group of samples were normalized to the levels in the corresponding control group; data are shown as averaged normalized values from five independent experiments. Statistical analysis was performed using one-way ANOVA and Sidak’s multiple comparisons test (GraphPad Prism 6). Results are presented as mean $\pm\:s.\mathrm{e.m}$ .
Animal procedures. All surgeries and behavioral tests were performed accord­ ing to NIH guidelines and approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC), the Institutional Biosafety Committee (IBC) and the Embryonic Stem Cell Research Committee (ESCRO). A total of 65 NOD-SCID $\mathrm{IL}2\mathrm{Rgc}$ null mice (NSG, $20{-}30~\mathrm{g};$ Jackson Laboratory) were transplanted for this study. Anesthesia was induced using ketamine ${\mathrm{90~mg/kg}};$ ; Akorn) and xylazine $(4~\mathrm{mg/kg})$ . For lesioning, 6-hydroxydopamine (6-OHDA, $10~{\upmu\mathrm{g}};$ Sigma-Aldrich) was injected into the right striatum at the following coor­ dinates (in millimeters from bregma): AP, $+0.5$ ; ML, $+2.0$ ; DV, $-3.0$ (from dura). Successfully lesioned mice $^{>6}$ amphetamine-induced rotations $/\mathrm{min}$ ) were selected for transplantation and randomized to receive either HALO or EYFP transplants. $2\times10^{5}$ cells were resuspended in $2~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of differentiation media and injected into the right striatum at the following coordinates (in mm from bregma): AP, $+0.5$ ; ML, $+2.0$ ; DV, $-3.2$ (from dura). Successfully recovered mice (an average of $_{<2}$ amphetamine-induced rotations per min 16 weeks post-grafting) were selected for cannula implantation and optoge­ netic testing. Implantable optical fibers (numerical aperture 0.39, $200\upmu\mathrm{m}$ core diameter, Thorlabs) were cut to a length of $2.5\mathrm{mm}$ , stereotactically inserted at the following coordinates (in mm from bregma): AP, $+0.5$ ; ML, $+2.0$ ; DV, $-2.2$ (from dura) and fixed on the skull with C&B-METABOND (Pearson Dental). Animals were allowed to recover from surgery for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ before the initiation of behavioral testing.
Tissue processing. Mice received pentobarbital (intraperitoneally $50\mathrm{mg/kg})$ ) to induce deep anesthesia and were transcardially perfused with phosphate buffered saline and then $4\%$ paraformaldehyde (PFA). Brains were extracted, post-fixed in $4\%$ PFA and then soaked in $30\%$ sucrose solutions for 1–5 days. They were sectioned on a cryostat after embedding in O.C.T. (Sakura-Finetek).
Cell counting. For the determination of in vitro cell fates, $3\:20\times$ fields with a minimum of 500 cells were counted manually from three independent dif­ ferentiation experiments. Data are presented as $\%$ of DAPI, TUJ or TH. For statistical comparison of HALO versus EYFP cell lines, unpaired two-tailed t-tests were performed assuming equal s.d. In vivo $\mathrm{TH^{+}}$ neurons were counted and calculated using the optical fractionator workflow in Stereo Investigator software (Neurolucida) with representative grafts for the HALO group con­ taining 8,775 and $6{,}750\mathrm{TH^{+}}$ neurons, which is similar to our published results for hESC-based mesDA neuron grafts2.
Behavioral assays. Amphetamine-induced rotations were assessed before transplantation as well as 8 and 16 weeks after transplantation. Animals with lesions only $\dot{}+{}$ sham graft) were included as negative controls alongside ani­ mals with EYFP and HALO grafts. Amphetamine-induced rotations were recorded starting $10~\mathrm{min}$ after intraperitoneal injection of d-amphetamine $\mathrm{\Omega_{\mathrm{10\mg/kg}}}$ , Sigma) for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The data are presented as average number of rotations per minute.
Electrophysiological recordings. Coronal striatal slices $250\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thick) were prepared from adult mice using a vibratome (Leica VT1200) and ice-cold cut­ ting solution containing (in mM): 100 glucose, $75\mathrm{{NaCl}}$ , $26\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}$ , $2.5\mathrm{KCl}$ $2~\mathrm{MgCl}_{2^{-}}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , $1.25\mathrm{\NaH_{2}P O_{4}{-}6H_{2}O}$ and $0.7\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ . Slices were allowed to recover in the solution for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $34^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then transferred to recording ACSF containing $(\mathrm{in~mM}$ ): 119 NaCl, $26.2\ \mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}$ , 10 glucose, $2.4\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ , $1.8~\mathrm{KCl}.$ , $1.2~\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ -6 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $1.0\ \mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4^{-6}}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ The temperature for recordings was set at $32~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(\pm2^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathrm{C})$ . For whole-cell patch clamp studies borosilicate glass pipettes with a tip resistance of 3–4 MΩ (G150F-4, Warner Instruments) were pulled on a P-97 Flaming-Brown micropipette puller (Sutter Instruments) and filled with: $115~\mathrm{mM}$ K-gluconate, $20~\mathrm{mM}$ KCl, $10~\mathrm{mM}$ HEPES, 2 mM $\mathrm{\MgCl}_{2}$ , $2\mathrm{mM}$ ATP-Mg, $2\mathrm{mM}$ ATP- $\cdot\mathrm{Na}_{2}$ and $0.3~\mathrm{mM}$ GTP-Na, $\mathrm{\Delta}\cdot\mathrm{PH}=7.25$ , ${\sim}280\mathrm{mOsm}$ ). For a subset of voltage-clamp studies, the pipette solution contained: $120\mathrm{mM}$ cesium-methanesulfonate, $11\mathrm{mM}$ glucose, $10\mathrm{mM}$ HEPES, $5\mathrm{mM}$ NaCl, 2 mM NaATP, 2 mM MgATP, $1.1\mathrm{mM}$ EGTA and $0.3\mathrm{mM}$ NaGTP $\left(\mathrm{pH}7.3,270-273\mathrm{mOsm}\right)$ . Grafted human neurons expressing HALO or EYFP were visualized under a $40\times$ water immersion objective by fluorescence and DIC optics (Olympus). Whole cell current clamp recording were performed with an Axopatch 200B amplifier (Molecular Devices) and digitized at $10\mathrm{kHz}$ with a Digidata 1332 or 1440A (Molecular Devices). Data were acquired using Clampex 10.2 software (Molecular Devices). In each cell, input resistance (measured by $100\mathrm{pA}$ , $100~\mathrm{{ms}}$ duration hyperpolarizing pulses), resting membrane potential and spontaneous firing frequencies were monitored throughout the recording. Only grafted cells with a stable baseline activity for 5 min were analyzed for tonic firing. All drugs were from Sigma unless otherwise specified. For optogenetic inhibition, a $560\mathrm{-nm}$ fluorescence lamp (Olympus) was used to achieve an illumination of $2\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ .
For measuring host-to-graft synaptic integration, ${\mathrm{EYFP^{+}}}$ dopamine neu­ rons were voltage clamped at $-60\mathrm{mV}.$ Excitatory post-synaptic currents were evoked by electrical stimulation $(100{-}400\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , $100\upmu\mathrm{sec}$ duration) driven by an ISO-flex stimulus isolator (AMPI) and Master-8 pulse generator (AMPI). A concentric bipolar tungsten electrode (World Precision Instruments) was placed within $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ of the recording site. Stable baseline excitatory postsynaptic currents were obtained for 5 min before starting baseline recording in the presence of $\mathrm{GABA_{A}}$ receptor antagonist picrotoxin $(50\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and NBQX $(10\upmu\mathrm{M})$ when indicated. Peak EPSC amplitudes were measured and sweeps were averaged for at least $5\mathrm{min}$ for each drug condition.
To examine the effect of the graft on striatal glutamatergic transmission, MSNs near the ${\mathrm{EYFP^{+}}}$ graft core or adjacent to ${\mathrm{EYFP^{+}}}$ processes were patch clamped and their input resistance and baseline resting membrane potential were monitored in the current clamp mode in the presence of $\mathrm{GABA_{A}}$ receptor antagonist picrotoxin $(50\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . If series resistance changed by $\pm20\%$ during the recording, data were discarded. Voltage-current relationships were measured by injecting step current from $-300$ to $+150\mathrm{pA}$ with $+50\mathrm{pA}$ increments. The excitability and the rheobase of MSNs were measured by injecting step current from 0 to $+150$ pA with $+10$ pA increments. For the assessment of glutama­ tergic transmission onto MSNs, excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs) were evoked every 20 s by electrical stimulation of the corpus callosum using a bipolar tungsten electrode as described previously48. When indicated, the D1 receptor antagonist SCH39166 was used at a concentration of $2\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Data were analyzed in Clampfit (Molecular Devices) and after baseline correction EPSP values were reported as mean $\pm\:\mathrm{s.e.m}$ . All experimental groups com­ prised a minimum of three animals. Statistical analysis was performed using the Kolmogorov–Smirnov normality test followed by one-way ANOVA and Sidak’s multiple comparisons test (GraphPad Prism 6).
Fast-scan cyclic voltammetry (CV) recordings were performed with cylin der ${5\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ carbon fiber electrodes (CFE) positioned at the dorsolateral striatum $50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ below the exposed surface. Striatal slices were electrically stimulated at the corpus callosum using a bipolar stainless steel electrode. Square pulses of $0.4\mathrm{-ms}$ duration were sent using an Iso-Flex stimulus isolator triggered by a Master-8 pulse generator (A.M.P.I.). Stimulus magnitude was selected by plotting a current–response curve and selecting the minimum value that pro­ duced the maximal response. Triangular voltage ramps from $-450\mathrm{mV}$ holding potential to $+800~\mathrm{{mV}}$ over $8.5~\mathrm{ms}$ (scan rate of $295~\mathrm{mV/ms}$ ) were applied to the CFE at 100-ms intervals. Current was recorded with an Axopatch 200B amplifier (Molecular Devices) filtered with $10\mathrm{-kHz}$ low-pass Bessel filter and digitized at $25\mathrm{kHz}$ (ITC-18 board; InstruTech). Triangular wave generation and data acquisition were controlled by a locally written computer routine in IGOR Pro (WaveMetrics). Background-subtracted cyclic voltammograms obtained in dopamine solutions of known concentration served to calibrate the electrodes and to identify released dopamine.
